<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ciudad Real</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ciudad Real is a city in Castile-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the capital of the province of the same name. King Alfonso X founded it in 1255 as Villa Real to strengthen royal power in La Mancha against the military orders, and it became a city in 1420. In antiquity its region lay between the lands of the Carpetani, Oretani and Olcades.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ciudad-real.jpg" alt="Ciudad Real" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zarateman · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ciudad_Real_-_Plaza_Mayor_04_edited.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ciudad Real lies in the southern half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meseta-central">Inner Plateau</a>, in the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, at about 625 metres above sea level. It belongs to the Campo de Calatrava, a transitional region between the Montes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a> that is unusual in the peninsula for its volcanic origin; the Plaza del Pilar is built at the centre of a shallow volcanic maar. No major river passes through the city, which lies between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a> to the north and its tributary the Jabalón to the south. It stands at the crossroads of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a> and Levante–Portugal routes but did not particularly prosper from this.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Villa Real, the Royal Town, was founded in 1255 under Alfonso X as a royal demesne town, with a charter modelled on that of Cuenca, over the hamlet of Pozuelo de San Gil. As an independent royal enclave within the dominion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-calatrava">Order of Calatrava</a>, it struggled at first to attract settlers, and the Order set up a rival market at nearby Miguelturra. Friction reached its climax about 1323 in armed conflict. A middle-sized jewry was documented by 1290, and the local aljama was largely dismantled in the pogroms of 1391. The town hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-castile">Cortes of Castile</a> in 1346.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-castile">Juan II of Castile</a> made it a city in 1420, with probably no more than 2,000 inhabitants. It became capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a> in 1691, and many of the buildings that followed were destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> earthquake of 1755. French troops occupied it in 1809. After the territorial division of 1833, Almagro and Manzanares challenged its status as provincial capital without success. Much of the centre was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ciudad Real is the capital of its province in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a>. Only two parts of the medieval wall stand, including the Toledo Gate. The Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> dates from the end of the 13th century and the Church of San Pedro from the 14th and 15th centuries. The cathedral, built in the 16th century, has the second-largest nave in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ciudad Real” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Francisco de Montejo the Younger made the city his capital, <b>Ciudad Real</b>, but the Maya besieged his force and he abandoned it in 1534.</blockquote><a href="/work/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The Carpetani occupied the upper and middle Tagus basin, in modern Madrid, Toledo and <b>Ciudad Real</b>, from the second half of the first millennium BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/carpetani">Carpetani</a></li><li><blockquote>The Olcades lived in the upper Guadiana and Júcar region of the southern Meseta, probably in modern Cuenca and <b>Ciudad Real</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/olcades">Olcades</a></li><li><blockquote>The Oretani lived in the upper Guadalquivir valley and the southern Meseta, in modern Jaén and <b>Ciudad Real</b>, from the mid-first millennium BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/oretani">Oretani</a></li></ul></section>
